## Diagnosis

The save/restore machinery was mostly intact — the scripted close→relaunch smoke (`--session-smoke`/`--restore-smoke`, driving the real window-close autosave path and the real `postLaunch`→`restoreLastSession` path under an isolated profile) proved URLs save and restore in order and pages actually load. The reproduced defect plus two latent ones:

1. **Saved active tab never selected** — `TabWidget::restoreState` called `setCurrentIndex(currentTab)` before creating the tabs, a no-op against the single placeholder tab; every restored session landed on tab 0.
2. **Silent tab drops** — a tab whose serialized history blob failed to parse was skipped entirely instead of falling back to its flat URL. For sessions written by the QtWebKit build (different per-tab history layout, same outer format) this dropped *every* tab — the most likely mechanism behind the "does not restore tabs" report.
3. **Crash-guard wedge** — `restoreLastSession` wrote `restoring=true` *before* validating the blob, so any parse failure left the flag set and replayed the "Arora crashed while restoring" prompt on every subsequent launch.

## Fixes

- `src/tabwidget.cpp` — current index applied (bounds-checked) after the restore loop; unreadable history blobs fall back to the flat saved URL; null-guarded `currentWebView()`.
- `src/browserapplication.cpp` — `restoring` flag now arms only after the blob validates; the crash-loop prompt semantics for a real mid-restore crash are unchanged.

## Verification

- `--session-smoke` (save phase): blob header, tab-state header, 3 URLs in order, index 1, private-window serialization empty, private-mode `saveSession` writes nothing — all PASS.
- `--restore-smoke` (relaunch): URLs in order, current index 1 restored, all three fixture pages loaded — PASS.
- `make check`: warnings gate PASS + all test programs 0 failures.

