## What shipped

**`PrivacyRequestInterceptor`** (`src/network/`) — a composite installed on the normal + private profiles (a profile takes exactly one interceptor, so the adblock matcher is wrapped inside it; the Tor profile keeps its stricter `TorRequestInterceptor`):
- **HTTPS-first**: `http:` main-frame navigations redirect to `https:`, skipping loopback, RFC1918/link-local/ULA IPs, `.localhost`/`.local`/`.onion`. A failed https load puts the host in a bounded (256) session downgrade set; the error page tells the user plain HTTP is allowed again for the session.
- **Referer spoof**: cross-site navigations carry the *target's* origin as `Referer`. The e2e proved the write works for navigations but is ignored for subresources (Chromium composes that header post-interception) — documented as an honest bound; subresources keep Chromium's origin-only policy.

**`BrowserProfile`** — `applyChromiumFlags()` runs in `main()` before `QtWebEngineProcess` spawns: `--force-webrtc-ip-handling-policy=disable_non_proxied_udp` (default on) and opt-in `--enable-features=DnsOverHttps`, appended without clobbering/duplicating user flags. `WebRTCPublicInterfacesOnly` is now tied to `privacy/webrtcIpProtection`.

**`CookieJar`** — `blockThirdPartyCookies` (default on) enforced in the IO-thread filter snapshot and the GUI-side `isAllowedForHost` mirror, with allow/session/block exception precedence preserved.

**Clear-on-exit** — opt-in `privacy/clearOnExit` wipes history, cookies, cache, downloads, search history and the saved session at quit, and drops `arora-exit-wipe.pending` so `Network`/`Cache`/`Code Cache` go away at next start (never under a running browser — the HARD01 wedge).

**Settings** — new "Connections & Storage" group on the Privacy tab with all five toggles plus a "Block third-party cookies" checkbox in the Cookies group.

## Verification

- `make check` green: check-warnings PASS + **51 test programs, 0 failed** offscreen
- New `autotests/privacy`: **27/27** (upgrade candidacy, downgrade set, cookie policy, deferred-wipe sentinels, flag composition) — with QSettings save/restore so the user's real config isn't touched
- `--privacy-smoke` (new loopback e2e over 127.0.0.1/127.0.0.2): PASS — http stayed un-upgraded, nav referer spoofed, first-party cookie kept, third-party rejected
- cookie/adblock/app/browser/settings smokes + `--quit-after-load`: all PASS
- Two regressions found by the suite and fixed: `installOnProfile` interceptor contract restored, omnibox test pinned `httpsFirst` off

README documents what an observer can still see (destination IPs, timing, fingerprint surface) and points at the Tor window for endpoint hiding. Stopping here per the one-task rule — the next feasible row is UA02.
