Now the info bar widget:The label is PlainText, so `SafeText::escaped` would show literal markup — fix to pass the raw host.Now I need to check how `loadSettings` propagates to all WebViews — important because a tier change (e.g. switching to Safest) must re-evaluate per-page JS attributes, since a page-level `true` would otherwise linger over the new profile-level `false`.`WebPage::loadSettings` is called for every live WebView on settings save — that's where the per-page policy gets re-applied on tier changes. Now the WebPage edits:Now WebView — host the info bar, forward the page signal, wire the allow buttons to a rule + reload:Now the SitePanel JavaScript row:Now the shield badge — badge dot when JS is blocked on the current page:Now wire the allow-snapshot into `shouldBlockScript` so an explicit grant bypasses the Safer http-script drop on the IO thread:Now surface the JS rules in the Settings audit list:Building to catch compile errors before writing the test:Build is clean and `--quit-after-load` exits 0. Now the autotest — checking `qtry.h` conventions first:Now the autotest — `autotests/scriptcontrol/`:Let me review the new JSCTL files before writing tests:Now let me verify the WebPage/WebView wiring in the current working tree:Now checking the interceptor + sitepanel + shield diffs:All integration looks solid. One bug I spotted on review: `updateScriptBlockBar` reads `url().host()`, but the signal fires inside `acceptNavigationRequest` — before the URL commits — so the bar would label the *previous* page's host. Fixing by tracking the blocked host on `WebPage`:Same consistency fix in `allowScriptsOnThisSite`:Now writing the autotest — `autotests/scriptcontrol/`:Test binary built. Running it headlessly:One failure — my own test caught a real semantics gap: with persistent `Allow` on `www.example.com` but `Block` on parent `example.com`, the deny-first ordering wins regardless of specificity. For this feature the more-specific rule should win (otherwise "Always allow" on a subdomain of a blocked domain silently does nothing). Fixing `ruleForHost` to pick the most specific match, session-beats-persistent and deny-beats-allow on ties:Rebuilding the test and re-running:All 23 pass. Now running the neighboring test targets that could regress (sitepanel, webpage, webview, privacy, settingsdialog, webpermissions, locationbar):All green. Now the full `make check`:Makefile regenerated. Running `make check` (warnings gate + full suite) in the background:`make check` exited 0. Let me confirm the warnings gate result and that `scriptcontrol` ran in the suite:`make check` fully green — zero warnings gate passed, all 54 test programs 0 failed including `tst_scriptcontrol` (23 cases). Now the task bookkeeping:All verification green. Now updating task docs — README's SECLVL/shield paragraphs need the per-site JS story:"Site Permissions" group is on the Privacy page — README accurate. Now the ChangeLog entry, task row, and WORKLOG:JSCTL complete — committed as `920c8ef` and pushed to `master`.

## What landed

**Core** — `ScriptControlManager` (src/scriptcontrolmanager.*): own QSettings map (`scriptcontrol` group) since the SEC05 broker keys on `QWebEnginePermission` types which have no JavaScript feature. Persistent rules + session overlay (Allow-once and off-the-record "always" never hit disk), parent-domain suffix matching, **most-specific rule wins** — a test-caught fix: deny-first ordering would have made "Always allow" on a subdomain of a blocked domain silently inert.

**Application** — `WebPage::applyJavaScriptPolicy()` sets the per-page `JavascriptEnabled` attribute inside `acceptNavigationRequest` before commit, and re-applies from `loadSettings()` so tier changes reach live tabs. Decision precedence: explicit Allow > explicit Block > Safest > Safer+http > global pref; internal schemes always allowed.

**UX** — `ScriptBlockInfoBar` ("Scripts blocked on `<host>`" + Allow once / Always allow, both reloading), shield icon badge + tooltip suffix, `SitePanel` JavaScript tri-state + live state line, and Settings > Site Permissions lists JS rules as `js|<host>` rows with remove/clear-all.

**Composition** — `PrivacyRequestInterceptor::shouldBlockScript` consults an IO-thread-safe allow snapshot so a granted site's *external* scripts aren't dropped by Safer's http block — the grant would otherwise be cosmetic.

