The exploration found the answer was path (a) all along: Qt ≥6.6 ships `QWebEngineGlobalSettings::setDnsMode`, which maps directly onto Chromium's `DnsOverHttpsMode`/`DnsOverHttpsTemplates`. PRIV01's "custom endpoint not exposed" bound was pre-API — the installed 6.12.0 has the header and the symbol.

What landed:

- **Settings**: the Secure-DNS checkbox became a mode selector — Off / Automatic upgrade / Custom server (system fallback) / Custom server (strict, no fallback) — plus an endpoint field defaulting to `https://cloudflare-dns.com/dns-query`.
- **`BrowserProfile::applySecureDns()`** — engine-global `setDnsMode` push, re-asserted from `applySettings()` so mode changes apply live. Invalid templates fall back to the default resolver rather than silently dropping a strict-mode user to plaintext DNS.
- **Migration**: `privacy/secureDnsMode` wins when present; the PRIV01-era `secureDns` bool maps to "automatic" when the mode key is absent and stays synced for older builds.
- **`--doh-smoke`** — three headless stages: strict + dead loopback endpoint must fail every lookup (`ERR_NAME_NOT_RESOLVED`, proving DNS is actually gated); strict + `1.1.1.1/dns-query` must load (SecureOnly ⇒ resolution could only come via DoH); mode → Off must restore system DNS at runtime. It saves/restores the user's real settings keys since `QSettings` ignores `QStandardPaths` test mode.
- **Honest bound kept**: CNAME-uncloaking documented in README — needs DNS-response inspection, no QtWebEngine hook exists.

