## What happened this run

**Root cause of the `--reader-smoke` crash:** stack-use-after-scope, not heap corruption. The smoke block's stage lambdas capture locals (`reader`, `pollUntil`, `stageNonArticle`, …) by reference, but `application.exec()` ran only at the bottom of `main()` — after the `if (--reader-smoke)` block had already destroyed them. Fixed by calling `return application.exec()` inside the block, and stripped all the debugging scaffolding (signal handlers, watchers, `READER_SMOKE_*` env-var bisection paths).

**Real product bug found once the harness ran:** `non-article refusal FAIL`. `Readability.parse()` is lenient enough to lift the stub page's lone `<p>short</p>` into an "article", so `enter()` activated on a non-article. Fixed by gating `enter()` on the same `probe()`/`isProbablyReaderable` heuristic that drives the icon — non-articles now refuse with the "not available" message.

**Test fix:** `tst_WebPage::webChannelForgedAutofillReport` asserted the channel object set `aroraAutofill,external`; `aroraReader` is now deliberately registered there (bridge exposes only `notifyExited`/`setPreference` — safe to expose). Updated the expectation.

## Verification

- `--reader-smoke` offscreen with `LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE=1`: **all 10 stages PASS** — probe, enter, overlay content, controls+prefs, DOM restore, JS-side exit, JS-blocked entry + flag restore, non-article probe + refusal.
- `make check`: `check-warnings` PASS (zero warnings), all autotest programs **0 failed**.

